-- Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two, four power armors (printed 94-106): Forester, Gladius Light, Grease Monkey, Ironwing
-- 4 vehicle rows, with 45 locations and 13 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~305-northern-gun-2-power-armor-d.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('ng-x33-forester', 'Forester Power Armor', 'rifts', 'power-armor', 'One.', NULL, 'Running: 60 mph (96 km) maximum at 10% of the normal fatigue rate, though in most woods and jungle half that is out of reach; through dense growth 15-20 mph (24 to 32 km) is the best it manages, it crashes audibly for one to three miles (1.6 to 4.8 km), and Perception Rolls, initiative and Land Navigation are halved while it does. Leaping: 15 ft (4.6 m) high and 20 ft (6.1 m) across; +30% with a running start. Climbing: +5% to the Climbing skill, as fast as the wearer''s own Spd attribute.', 'None.', 'Not built for water; swims 20 mph (32 km/17.3 knots) or walks the bottom at 10 mph (16 km/8.6 knots), 20% of the normal fatigue rate. Maximum depth 800 ft (244 m).', 'Man-sized: height 6-8 ft (1.8 to 2.4 m); width 2-3 ft (0.6 to 0.9 m); length 2-3 ft (0.6 to 0.9 m)', '372 lbs (167.4 kg)', 175, 1, NULL, 570000, '1.1 million credits nuclear, 780,000 Solid Oxide, 570,000 electric battery; the lowest is stored. Separately priced: Hex grenades at 2,500 each (gear row ng-hex-grenade), smoke flares at 80 each (ng-x33-smoke-flare), replacement electronic markers at 200 each (ng-x33-electronic-trail-marker) and UV paint refills at 20 a canister (ng-x33-uv-paint-refill). One Hex cloud load for the shoulder dispenser costs 2,000 credits.', 'Model Type: NG-X33. Class: Wilderness Reconnaissance Power Armor. Built for scouting, pathfinding and cutting trails through forest rather than for battle: a team under James Unick, Rory Evavold, Brenda Hicks and Alex K. Smith designed it for lumber camps, trappers, big game hunters, cartographers, Wilderness Scouts and the mercenaries who followed them, and NG warns buyers it is no combat suit. Sold in olive green or forest camouflage. Trail gear: a Vibro-Chainsaw on the left arm for clearing brush, felling small trees, building blinds and carving arrows into bark or rock; a napalm flamethrower on the right forearm, fed from a back tank, for controlled burns, scaring off animals and torching monster lairs or Xiticix hatcheries; a square nozzle above the left wrist that sprays ultraviolet paint (2 ft/0.6 m reach, a 12 ounce can''s worth) for marks only black light or UV optics reveal; and two neck collar compartments holding ten electronic markers each, small green clip-on beacons that ping on an unlikely radio band, rising in pitch as a follower closes in, for one mile (1.6 km) over ten days. Twelve colored smoke flares ride on a bandoleer across the left side; each sends a thin column 400 ft (122 m) up for 20 minutes plus 2D4 minutes of fading trail (half that in winds over 8 mph/13 km). Climbing aids: a grappling hook with 50 ft (15.2 m) of cord under each forearm, a retractable lineman''s loop belt for hanging in a tree as a lookout or sniper, rubber-gripped palms, and feet with retractable cleats and six flip-down climbing spikes each. A tiny peekaboo camera unfolds silently from the right shoulder on a thin arm to watch behind the wearer through leaves and feed his HUD, so he can stay perfectly still; it is usually paired with the flip-up laser on the back of the helmet, which has its own aiming camera, to get the first shot at something creeping up behind. Cat-like landings: drops to 60 ft (18.3 m) are safe; from 60 to 120 ft (36.6 m) roll 1D20, on 1-10 the suit takes 2D4x10 M.D., the wearer 6D6 S.D.C./Hit Points and is stunned 1D4 melee rounds, on 11-20 the suit takes 5D6 M.D., the wearer 2D6 S.D.C. and loses two melee actions; higher drops are not survivable. Robot P.S. 26. Cargo: none, though belts, pouches, holsters and a backpack or satchel are common. Power: nuclear, 12 year average life; Solid Oxide and electric battery versions are sold. A handheld NG energy pistol and an NG laser or ion rifle come standard. Hand to hand per Ground-Based Power Armor Training, Basic or Elite (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.351-352): restrained punch 1D4 M.D., full punch 1D6, power punch 2D6 (two attacks), kick 2D4 (+1 with cleats, +1D4 with spikes), leap kick 2D8 (+2 with cleats, +1D4+2 with spikes; two attacks). Features beyond the standard: a black light on the right hand''s back (3 ft/0.9 m); two LED hand lights on the left hand (40 ft/12.2 m); two temple cameras and two on the left hand that record only on command (96 hours of video, 8,000 stills); a helmet head lamp (100 ft/30.5 m); and multi-optics with passive nightvision and infrared (1,600 ft/488 m), ultraviolet (400 ft/122 m), telescopic sight (2 miles/3.2 km), x12 macro and a camera of 100 hours and 4,000 stills.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.94-99'),
  ('ng-ex10-gladius-light', 'Gladius Light Power Armor', 'rifts', 'power-armor', 'One.', NULL, 'Running: 40 mph (64 km) maximum, 30% of the normal fatigue rate at high speed, 20% at 20 mph (32 km) or less. Leaping: 10 ft (3 m) high or across; +30% with a running start.', 'None.', 'None; it sinks like a rock.', 'Man-sized, to the buyer''s size: height 5-7.6 ft (1.5 to 2.3 m); width 2-3 ft (0.6 to 0.9 m); length 1.6-4 ft (0.4 to 1.2 m)', '80-104 lbs (36 to 46 kg), by the buyer''s size', 180, 1, NULL, 150000, '150,000 credits for the complete suit, electric battery only. Extra-cost options: helmet light 1,200 (gear row ng-ex10-helmet-light), head cam 3,500 (ng-ex10-head-cam), built-in language translator 10,000 (ng-ex10-language-translator) and voice modulation 13,500 (ng-ex10-voice-modulation).', 'Model Type: NG-EX10. Class: Armored Infantry Light Exo-Skeleton. One of Northern Gun''s earliest man-sized suits, styled after the popular Samson helmet but with only bare-bones robotics, and still built unchanged because it keeps selling: a dinosaur beside modern power armor, it cannot fly or outrun most vehicles, but it adds speed, strength and far more protection than unpowered body armor, which makes it the starter heavy armor of new adventurers and mercenaries. Nothing that draws on the suit''s power can be built into it, since that would empty the battery in minutes. Power: a rechargeable electric battery, never nuclear, good for 96 hours of steady running (4-12 days in practice) and recharged in 2 hours from any vehicle or facility with a nuclear plant or generator. Robot P.S. 20. Cargo: none. Weapons: none built in; the wearer carries rifles, pistols, shotguns, Vibro-Blades or light rail guns, with more on belts and chest straps or in a backpack and satchels. Hand to hand: only the bonuses of Robot (and Power Armor) Combat: Basic (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352) apply in this suit, whatever the wearer''s other training; the Elite training does not count. Restrained punch 2D6 S.D.C., full punch 1D4 M.D., power punch 2D4 (two actions), kick 1D4, leap kick 2D6 (two actions). Features: only those of ordinary Environmental Body Armor (EBA, this book p.52), not power armor, plus the priced options: a miner''s-lamp helmet light, a dashboard-style head cam, a helmet language translator, and a mouthpiece loudspeaker with translator and 20-voice modulator.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.99-100'),
  ('ng-mru886-grease-monkey', 'Grease Monkey Power Armor', 'rifts', 'power-armor', 'One.', NULL, 'Running: 40 mph (64 km) maximum, 20% of the normal fatigue rate. Leaping: 10 ft (3 m) high or across unassisted, +30% with a running start, doubled with thruster assist. Hover Lift Thrusters (special): jets in the feet and legs lift it up to 28 ft (8.5 m) to work on big vehicles and giant robots, with small steadying jets at the legs, knees, hips, under the ribs and behind the shoulders; one or two can reload a big robot''s weapons in one or two melee rounds without a ladder or lift. No power jumping travel.', 'None; the hover system only raises and holds it stationary to work, to 28 ft (8.5 m).', 'Swims 10 mph (16 km/8.6 knots), 15 mph (24 km/13 knots) with thruster assist; walks the bottom at 4 mph (6.4 km/3.4 knots). Maximum depth 1,500 ft (457 m). Not sold as amphibious, but it moves between land and water freely and works on hulls, props and rudders underwater.', 'Man-sized, to the buyer''s size: height 6-8 ft (1.8 to 2.4 m); width 3-3.5 ft (0.9 to 1 m); length 2.5-3.5 ft (0.76 to 1 m)', '356 lbs (160 kg)', 185, 1, NULL, 625000, '1.3 million credits nuclear (printed as 1.3 credits), 975,000 Solid Oxide, 625,000 electric; the lowest is stored.', 'Model Type: NG-MRU886. Class: Strategic Armored Combat Suit and Mechanical Repair Support. A mobile field repair unit from a team under Kevin McGoldrick, Mark Muller, Frank Stoesser and Carl Gleba, meant to get disabled robots, power armor and vehicles moving again under fire. The pilot should be an Operator, or work under one, with at least basic mechanical and electrical skill, and the suit gives such mechanics better protection than EBA. It has no built-in weapons at all and relies on combat units to guard it. An instant hit with militaries and civilians alike, it turns up with adventurers, troops, ships, bases, Black Market shops, town garages and wilderness towing outfits, bandits and pirates included. Equipment: three 2 gallon (7.5 liter) tanks wrapped around the left thigh, of industrial grease, a solvent that strips organic residue, and a coolant-leak sealer, each with a spray or injection head; a hidden right-leg compartment of small spare parts (circuit boards, chips, fuses, bypass connectors, coolant and hydraulic fittings), often stocked for the machines its Operator maintains; finger tools and connectors that download a robot''s diagnostic logs; a computer that shows schematics and repair or jury-rig plans on the HUD, flagging damage red and trouble spots yellow, and can override a machine''s safety lockouts at some risk; jumper cables and tools in the arms; a laser eye in the helmet for cutting and soldering; and a UEL (Universal Energy Link) in each hip to run handheld energy weapons or tools off the suit. An outsized hard-shell pack (2.5 times the standard EBA one) carries more tools and parts. A handheld NG energy pistol and a Vibro-Knife (1D4 M.D.) are standard; an energy rifle or shotgun may be carried, but nothing two-handed or very heavy. Cat-like landings: drops to 60 ft (18.3 m) are safe; from 60 to 120 ft (36.6 m) roll 1D20, on 1-10 the suit takes 2D4x10 M.D., the wearer 6D6 S.D.C./Hit Points and is stunned 1D4 melee rounds, on 11-20 the suit takes 5D6 M.D., the wearer 2D6 S.D.C. and loses two melee actions; higher drops do double the 1-10 result and may kill. Robot P.S. 28. Cargo: none. Power: nuclear, 10 year life; Solid Oxide and electric models run 96 hours per full charge. Hand to hand per Ground-Based Power Armor Training, Basic or Elite (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.351-352): restrained punch 1D4 M.D., full punch 1D6, power punch 2D6 (two attacks), kick 2D4, leap kick 2D8 (two attacks). Features beyond the standard: basic sonar tracking 36 targets in a 10 mile (16 km) radius underwater; a depth gauge whose alarm starts at 1,300 ft (396 m) and counts down aloud; a helmet head lamp (100 ft/30.5 m); multi-optics with passive nightvision and infrared (1,600 ft/488 m), ultraviolet (400 ft/122 m), telescopic sight (2 miles/3.2 km), x20 macro and a camera of 100 hours and 4,000 stills; a 5 M.D.C. helmet searchlight (300 ft/91.4 m); and two shuttered high-intensity chest lights for working inside machines.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.100-103'),
  ('ng-xf17-ironwing', 'Ironwing Flying Power Armor', 'rifts', 'power-armor', 'One.', NULL, 'Running: 40 mph (64 km) maximum, 50 mph (80 km) with both wings gone, 20% of the normal fatigue rate; the big wings make running slow. Leaping: 10 ft (3 m) high or 15 ft (4.6 m) across unassisted; jet-assisted up to 100 ft (30.5 m) high and 200 ft (61 m) across without flight.', 'VTOL, hover and flight: 290 mph (464 km) maximum, cruising 100-150 mph (160 to 240 km), ceiling 4,000 ft (1,219 m). The jets must cool after at most eight hours of continuous flight; with rest stops every 2-5 hours it flies indefinitely. +1 to strike with the wing blades and +2 to dodge in flight.', 'No aquatic propulsion; submersion knocks out the jets until they dry (1D4+2 hours). It can swim or walk the bottom at 5 mph (8 km). Maximum depth 800 ft (244 m).', 'Oversized human: height 8-10 ft (2.4 to 3 m), the wearer''s feet in its ankles about 2 ft (0.6 m) up; width 4 ft (1.2 m) across the launchers, 7 ft (2.1 m) with wings folded; wingspan 19 ft (5.8 m); length 3 ft (0.9 m)', '720 lbs (320 kg); a second Weight line printing 372 lbs (167.4 kg) repeats the Forester''s figure', 235, 1, NULL, 1500000, '2.5 million credits nuclear, 2 million Solid Oxide, 1.5 million electric battery; the lowest is stored.', 'Model Type: NG-XF17. Class: Armored Flying Infantry Assault Suit. A big, menacing flyer from the Dragon Blaze Ninety-Nine team (Lawrence Scarlet, Jeremy Hurraw and Talor Swanchel), drawing on the Coalition SAMAS and Triax Predator. It serves as an all-round combat flyer but was aimed at Xiticix, Gargoyles, flying demons and dragons, with weapons for dogfights, strafing and air-to-ground (or air-to-hive) attacks. Faster than any Xiticix, it can outfly a swarm. Unlike the smaller Red Hawk it is tough and well armed enough for drawn-out melee. Two large curved wings fold down and back on the ground and spread level with the shoulders in flight, where it flies upright like the SAMAS; the 19 ft (5.8 m) span forces it to land and walk in tight spaces such as narrow hive tunnels, and folded it must turn sideways for most doors. The wing edges are sharpened silver-plated blades, and clipping enemy flyers with them at modest speed is a common tactic. Losing one wing grounds it and unbalances it (-20% to Acrobatics, Climbing, Gymnastics, Prowl and Swimming, -20% speed and leaping) until the other is jettisoned; jet-assisted leaps still work. On sale only since fall 109 P.A., it already sells well to warriors, mercenary companies and young kingdoms without an air force. Robot P.S. 28. Cargo: none. Power: nuclear, 12 year average life (the flying range line says 15); Solid Oxide and electric battery versions are sold. Two UELs (Universal Energy Links) hide under plating in the right forearm and cable to a handheld energy weapon for unlimited shots; any handheld weapon may be used, and belts and chest straps carry a side arm, Vibro-Blade, grenade reloads, hand and smoke grenades and flares. Hand to hand per Basic or Elite Flying Power Armor Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.351-352): restrained punch 1D4 M.D., full punch 1D6, power punch 2D6 (two attacks), power dive punch 3D6 (two attacks, and every combat bonus but strike halved, roll with impact included), kick 2D4, leap kick 2D8 (two attacks). Features beyond the standard: radar tracking 96 airborne targets to 100 miles (160 km); a language translator holding the nine known languages of the Americas plus room for 12, following three voices and two languages at once (98.7% accurate with a three second delay for one speaker, 78% and six seconds for three); a telescopic helmet camera (2 miles/3.2 km; 100 hours of video, 4,000 stills); passive nightvision (2,000 ft/610 m) with infrared and ultraviolet (1,000 ft/305 m); and a loudspeaker with 20-voice modulator.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.103-106');

